A = {
100: "CMOS (complementary MOS) builds each gate from a PMOS pull-up network to VDD and an NMOS pull-down network to ground that are logical complements, so for any input exactly one network conducts. NMOS passes a strong 0 and PMOS a strong 1, so together the output swings fully rail to rail. In steady state there is no path from VDD to ground, so static power is almost zero (only leakage). That full swing, high noise margin and low power are why CMOS dominates digital logic.",
101: "Start from the camera's data rate: pixel clock x bits per pixel, for example 1920 x 1080 x 30 fps x 16 bits = about 1 Gbit/s. Capture the camera bus (parallel DVP or MIPI CSI-2 lanes) in the camera's clock domain, cross into the system clock with an asynchronous FIFO sized for line bursts, then write frames to external DRAM through a DMA or AXI master with burst writes and double (ping-pong) buffering, so one frame is read while the next is written. Check that memory bandwidth covers both write and read traffic with margin, and add line buffers if processing needs neighbouring rows.",
102: "A pipeline splits instruction execution into stages (fetch, decode, execute, memory, write-back) so several instructions are in flight at once and throughput approaches one instruction per cycle; hazards are handled by forwarding, stalls and branch prediction. A cache is a small fast memory near the core that holds recently used lines; on a hit data returns in a few cycles, on a miss the line is fetched from the next level. Caches keep the memory stage of the pipeline from stalling on slow DRAM.",
103: "The program uses virtual addresses. On each access the MMU looks up the TLB to translate the virtual page number to a physical frame number; on a TLB miss it walks the page tables in memory (often through the caches) and fills the TLB, checking permissions on the way. The physical address (or, in a VIPT L1, the virtual index plus the physical tag) then looks up the caches. Virtual memory provides isolation and paging to disk; the TLB and caches make the translation and data access fast.",
104: "A stall freezes the earlier stages and inserts a bubble when an instruction cannot proceed. The forwarding unit compares the source registers of the instruction in EX with the destination registers of instructions in MEM and WB, and muxes their results straight into the ALU inputs, so most RAW hazards need no stall. The hazard detection unit in ID catches the case forwarding cannot fix, a load followed by an instruction that uses its result: it stalls the PC and the IF/ID register for one cycle and turns the ID/EX control into a bubble.",
105: "Structural hazards: two instructions need the same hardware in the same cycle (for example one memory port for instructions and data). Data hazards: an instruction depends on a result not yet available (RAW, plus WAR and WAW in out-of-order machines). Control hazards: the next instruction address is unknown until a branch resolves. They are handled with extra resources, forwarding, stalls, register renaming, and branch prediction with flushing.",
106: "In a non-pipelined (single-cycle or multi-cycle) design, one instruction goes through all steps before the next starts, so the time per instruction is the sum of all stage delays. In a pipelined design, each stage works on a different instruction every cycle; a single instruction still takes about the same total latency (slightly more because of pipeline registers), but a new instruction completes every cycle, so throughput rises by up to the number of stages, minus hazard stalls.",
107: "On a miss, the cache controller allocates a line (choosing a victim with the replacement policy and writing it back if it is dirty), then sends a read request for the whole line, aligned to the line size, to the next level or the memory controller. The data comes back in bursts, often critical word first so the waiting load can continue early; the line is written into the data array, the tag and valid bit are updated, and the pending requests in the MSHR are completed.",
108: "L1 is the smallest and fastest cache, closest to the core (typically 32 to 64 KB, a few cycles of latency), usually split into separate instruction and data caches. L2 is larger (hundreds of KB to a few MB) and slower (around 10 to 15 cycles), usually unified, and catches L1 misses. L1 is optimised for hit time, L2 for hit rate; they can be inclusive or exclusive of each other, and an L3 shared by all cores often sits beyond them.",
109: "Structural hazards are resolved by duplicating resources (separate instruction and data memories) or stalling. Data hazards are resolved by forwarding results from later stages, stalling for load-use, and in out-of-order cores by register renaming and reservation stations. Control hazards are resolved by branch prediction, resolving branches early, delay slots in older ISAs, and flushing the wrong-path instructions on a misprediction.",
110: "Pipelining overlaps the stages of consecutive instructions, like an assembly line, so one instruction can complete per cycle and the clock can be faster because each stage is short. The problems: hazards (structural, data, control) that cause stalls and need forwarding and prediction logic; branch mispredict penalties that grow with depth; extra pipeline-register area, power and latency; and more complex exception handling and verification.",
111: "Pipelining splits a long operation into stages separated by registers so that several data items or instructions are processed at once. In an SoC it is used everywhere: CPU pipelines, DSP and video datapaths, bus interconnects and memory controllers. It raises the achievable clock frequency (shorter combinational paths) and throughput, at the cost of latency and area, and it is the main tool for closing timing on long paths.",
112: "Small, fast caches sit close to the core and larger, slower ones further away: L1 instruction and data caches per core, an L2 per core or cluster, and a shared last-level cache (L3 or system cache), then DRAM. Each level filters misses from the one above. The policies (inclusive or exclusive, write-back, coherence between cores) are chosen to balance hit latency, hit rate, area and power.",
113: "Storage arranged from fastest, smallest and most expensive per bit to slowest, largest and cheapest: registers, L1, L2 and L3 caches (SRAM), main memory (DRAM), then flash or SSD and disk. Locality means most accesses hit the upper levels, so the system appears to have the speed of the top and the capacity of the bottom.",
114: "The MMU translates virtual addresses to physical addresses using page tables, with a TLB to cache translations. It enforces protection (read, write, execute and user or kernel permissions) so processes cannot touch each other's memory, raises page faults so the OS can load pages on demand, and sets memory attributes such as cacheable or device memory. It enables virtual memory, process isolation and shared libraries.",
115: "Virtual memory gives each process its own address space that is mapped onto physical memory and backed by disk. It is implemented mostly with paging: memory is divided into fixed-size pages, page tables (often multi-level, or inverted or hashed) map virtual pages to physical frames, and a TLB caches the mappings. Segmentation, which maps variable-size segments with base and limit registers, is the older alternative, sometimes combined with paging.",
116: "Direct-mapped caches are fastest and simplest but suffer conflict misses; set-associative caches (2 to 16 ways) reduce conflicts at some cost in hit time and power; fully associative caches have no conflicts but are only practical when small (TLBs). Split versus unified, and write-back versus write-through, are other choices. No type is best in general: L1 caches favour low associativity for speed, while L2 and L3 favour higher associativity for hit rate.",
117: "Virtual memory lets programs use a large, contiguous address space independent of the physical memory size and layout. The advantages: isolation and protection between processes, more programs than fit in RAM (paging to disk), simpler linking and loading because every program can use the same addresses, shared memory and shared libraries, copy-on-write, and memory-mapped files.",
118: "Pipelining raises throughput: with k stages, ideally one instruction completes every cycle instead of every k cycles, and each stage being shorter allows a higher clock. Hardware is also better used, because every stage is busy. The cost is hazards, extra registers and more complex control, but the performance gain is large, which is why every modern processor is pipelined.",
119: "An n-bit address bus can address 2^n locations. With byte addressing that is 2^n bytes: 16 bits give 64 KB, 32 bits give 4 GB, and 40 bits give 1 TB. If each address points to a wider word, multiply by the word size.",
120: "Balance the two paths. If the clock path has extra latency, the capture clock arrives late: that helps setup but hurts hold, so add delay on the data path (buffers) to fix hold, or reduce the skew by balancing the clock tree. If the data path has extra latency, it causes setup violations: shorten it (faster cells, restructuring, retiming, pipelining) or add useful skew by delaying the capture clock, provided the next stage's hold still passes.",
121: "Give DV a specification that is testable: the features, the interface protocol and its timing, the registers, the error cases and the corner cases you are worried about (full, empty, overflow, reset in the middle of a transaction, back-pressure). List the outputs and internal status signals they must check, the invariants worth writing as assertions (handshakes, one-hot states, counters in range), and the coverage you expect. Review their test plan together.",
122: "No. Dynamic power grows with C V^2 f, and a higher frequency usually needs a higher voltage, so power and heat grow roughly with the cube of frequency (the power wall). Wire delay does not scale with transistor size, and shorter cycles mean more pipeline stages with larger branch penalties. Memory latency does not improve at the same rate, so a faster core just waits longer. That is why industry moved to more cores, wider and specialised units instead of higher clock rates.",
123: "Dynamic voltage and frequency scaling (DVFS) to match performance to load; clock gating to stop clocks to idle logic; power gating to switch off idle blocks with header or footer switches (with isolation and state retention); multiple voltage domains; multi-Vt cell selection to cut leakage; low-power states and sleep modes controlled by a power-management unit; and at software level, race-to-idle and scheduling. These are described in UPF or CPF for implementation and verification.",
124: "A mobile SoC has clusters of Arm CPU cores (big and little), a GPU, an NPU for AI, a DSP and an image signal processor, video codecs, display and camera interfaces, a modem, security and boot blocks, a system cache, and memory controllers for LPDDR. They connect through a coherent interconnect (CCI or CMN using AMBA ACE/CHI) for the CPUs and IO-coherent masters, and AXI, AHB and APB buses for peripherals, with SMMUs for device address translation, plus clock, reset and power-management controllers.",
125: "The control unit (fetch, decode, sequencing and branch handling), the datapath (register file, ALU, shifter, multiplier, FPU), pipeline registers, the program counter, load-store units with the memory interface, caches and TLBs, and in high-performance cores branch predictors, rename logic, reservation stations and a reorder buffer. Interrupt and exception logic and a bus interface connect it to the rest of the system.",
126: "Parallel: data bus N bits wide, plus valid and ready (or req and ack); the sender drives data and valid, and a transfer happens on a clock edge when both valid and ready are high, so back-pressure is natural. Serial: one data line plus a clock (SPI-like) with a frame signal marking the start, shifting MSB first; or asynchronous like a UART with a start bit, N data bits, parity and a stop bit at an agreed baud rate. Discuss throughput, pin count, error detection and clock-domain issues.",
127: "A classic Arm core is a RISC load-store design: fixed-length 32-bit instructions (plus 16-bit Thumb), a large register file (31 general registers in AArch64), conditional execution or branches, and simple addressing modes, all of which make decode and pipelining easy. Implementations range from small in-order pipelines (Cortex-M, 3 stages) to wide out-of-order cores (Cortex-A and X) with branch prediction, renaming, multiple execution pipes, NEON SIMD, an MMU and caches, connected through AMBA buses.",
128: "Draw the classic five stages: Fetch (PC addresses the instruction memory; PC + 4 or a branch target), Decode (read the register file, generate control signals, sign-extend the immediate), Execute (ALU operation or address calculation; branch comparison), Memory (load or store to the data memory), and Write-back (write the result to the register file). Follow one instruction, such as an add or a load, through each stage, showing the pipeline registers between stages and the forwarding paths.",
129: "Draw the CPU cluster with its caches, a GPU and other accelerators, all on a central interconnect (NoC or AMBA). Behind the interconnect: the memory controller to DRAM, on-chip SRAM, a DMA engine, and a peripheral bus bridge to UART, SPI, I2C, GPIO and timers. Add high-speed IO (PCIe, USB, Ethernet), the clock and reset generator with PLLs, the power-management controller, an interrupt controller, a boot ROM and security block, and the debug and JTAG port.",
130: "Front end: PC, branch predictor and BTB, instruction cache and TLB, fetch queue and decoders. For an out-of-order core: rename map and physical register file, dispatch into reservation stations or issue queues, multiple execution units (ALUs, branch, multiply, FPU and SIMD, load-store units with a load-store queue and data cache), a reorder buffer for in-order retirement, and the L2 cache interface. For an in-order core, draw the five-stage pipeline with forwarding.",
131: "Branch prediction guesses the outcome and target of a branch before it executes, so the pipeline keeps fetching. Static prediction (backward taken, forward not taken) is simplest. Dynamic predictors use 2-bit saturating counters per branch, correlate with global or local history (gshare, two-level, tournament, TAGE), use a branch target buffer for targets and a return address stack for returns. On a mispredict the wrong-path instructions are flushed, costing roughly the pipeline depth in cycles.",
132: "Dynamic (switching) power is C V^2 f alpha, from charging and discharging load capacitance: reduce it by clock gating, lower activity (operand isolation, encoding), lower voltage and frequency (DVFS), and smaller capacitance. Short-circuit power flows briefly while both PMOS and NMOS are on during slow input transitions: reduce it with sharp input slews and balanced sizing. Static (leakage) power flows even when idle, through subthreshold, gate and junction leakage: reduce it with high-Vt cells, power gating, body biasing and lower voltage.",
133: "An in-order processor issues instructions in program order, so one stalled instruction (for example waiting on a cache miss) blocks everything behind it. An out-of-order processor lets later independent instructions execute first, keeping execution units busy, and commits results in order through a reorder buffer. Register renaming makes this possible by mapping architectural registers to more physical registers, removing the false WAR and WAW dependencies caused by register reuse.",
134: "In saturation ID = K (VGS - Vt)^2. The ratio gives 325 / 87.62 = 3.709, so (12 - Vt) / (7 - Vt) = sqrt(3.709) = 1.926. Then 12 - Vt = 13.48 - 1.926 Vt, so Vt = 1.48 / 0.926 = about 1.6 V. Then K = 325 mA / (12 - 1.6)^2 = 325 / 108.2 = about 3.0 mA/V^2. (If the model uses ID = (K/2)(VGS - Vt)^2, K is twice that, about 6 mA/V^2.)",
135: "Turn the timing diagram into a specification: input-to-output behaviour, latency, protocol rules and reset values. Build a reference model of the expected behaviour, drive directed tests reproducing the diagram, then random stimulus within the protocol rules, and compare outputs with the model in a scoreboard. Add assertions for the timing relationships (for example ack within N cycles of req) and coverage for every input combination and protocol phase.",
136: "Write C tests that run on the processor model inside the simulation (or on emulation or FPGA): the C code configures the subsystem's registers, starts operations, polls or takes interrupts, and checks results in memory. A mailbox or a reserved memory location reports pass or fail and debug prints to the testbench. Combine with SystemVerilog monitors and scoreboards on the buses, and use the same tests later for post-silicon validation.",
137: "Masters start transactions (CPU, DMA, GPU); slaves respond (memory, peripherals, register blocks); bridges and the interconnect are slaves on one side and masters on the other. The number of addresses in a range is end - start + 1: for example 0x4000_0000 to 0x4000_FFFF is 0x10000 = 65,536 byte addresses (64 KB). Check the decoder: ranges must not overlap, and unmapped addresses should return an error response.",
138: "Split the string on commas and convert the three fields to integers n, i and j. Get bit i with (n >> i) & 1 and bit j with (n >> j) & 1 (or index the binary string bin(n)[2:] from the right), compare them and print True or False. In Python: n, i, j = map(int, s.split(',')); print(((n >> i) & 1) == ((n >> j) & 1)). Handle bad input and bit indexes beyond the number's width.",
139: "A semaphore is a synchronisation object holding a count of available keys. get or wait decrements it and blocks if no key is available; put or signal increments it and wakes a waiter. A binary semaphore (one key) gives mutual exclusion over a shared resource; a counting semaphore limits access to N resources. In SystemVerilog testbenches, semaphores make sure, for example, that only one thread drives a shared bus at a time.",
140: "A RISC load-store architecture with fixed-length instructions; a large register file (31 general registers in AArch64); simple addressing modes; conditional execution (AArch32) and condition flags; the compact Thumb instruction set; NEON and SVE SIMD; TrustZone security; exception levels EL0 to EL3 for user, OS, hypervisor and secure monitor; a weakly ordered memory model with barriers; and the AMBA bus ecosystem. Its strength is performance per watt and a licensing model used across mobile, embedded and servers.",
141: "The memory management unit translates the virtual addresses used by software into physical addresses using page tables, caching recent translations in a TLB. It also enforces access permissions per page (read, write, execute, user or privileged), sets memory attributes such as cacheability, and raises page faults or exceptions on illegal or unmapped accesses, which enables virtual memory and process isolation.",
142: "An abstract class cannot be instantiated; it defines a common interface (often with pure virtual methods) that derived classes must implement. In SystemVerilog you write virtual class; in C++ a class with a pure virtual function. A shallow copy copies the object's members, but handles inside it still point to the same sub-objects, so both copies share them; a deep copy also creates new copies of the contained objects, so the copies are fully independent. In SystemVerilog, new with an existing handle does a shallow copy; deep copy needs a custom copy() method.",
143: "A control unit, a datapath (register file, ALU, shifter, multiplier and FPU), the program counter and fetch logic, decode logic, pipeline registers, load-store units, caches and TLBs, a bus interface, and interrupt and exception handling. High-end cores add branch predictors, register renaming, schedulers, a reorder buffer and SIMD units.",
144: "Paging divides virtual memory into fixed-size pages (often 4 KB) and physical memory into frames of the same size. A page table maps each virtual page to a frame, with valid, permission and dirty bits; multi-level tables keep the table small. A TLB caches recent mappings. If a page is not in memory, a page fault lets the OS load it from disk. Paging avoids external fragmentation and makes allocation simple.",
145: "The memory management unit is the hardware between the CPU and memory that translates virtual addresses to physical ones using page tables and a TLB, checks access permissions, and reports faults to the operating system. It is what makes virtual memory, protection between processes and demand paging possible.",
146: "Transit time is roughly the channel length divided by the carrier velocity: t = L / (mu E) = L^2 / (mu VDS). With L = 180 nm = 1.8e-5 cm, mu = 750 cm^2/V.s and VDS = 3 V: t = (1.8e-5)^2 / (750 x 3) = 3.24e-10 / 2250 = about 1.4e-13 s, so around 0.14 ps. In a real short-channel device the carriers hit velocity saturation (about 1e7 cm/s), giving L / vsat = about 1.8 ps.",
147: "Clock gating, multi-Vt cells (high-Vt where timing allows), power gating with isolation and retention, multiple voltage domains and level shifters, dynamic voltage and frequency scaling, operand isolation, memory banking and low-power memory modes, reducing switching activity (Gray coding, avoiding glitches, sharing logic), and architecture choices such as parallelism at lower voltage. Power intent is captured in UPF.",
148: "Cast the address to a pointer to volatile and dereference it, so the compiler always performs the access: uint32_t read_reg(uintptr_t addr) { return *(volatile uint32_t *)addr; }. volatile prevents the compiler from caching or removing the read, which matters for memory-mapped hardware registers. Make sure the address is aligned to the access size.",
149: "Physical design turns the synthesized netlist into a layout. Floorplanning sets the die size, places macros and IO, and builds the power grid. Placement puts standard cells to minimise wire length and congestion while meeting timing. Clock tree synthesis builds a balanced clock network with controlled skew. Routing connects every net with metal layers. Then sign-off: STA with extracted parasitics, IR drop and EM, signal integrity, DRC, LVS and antenna checks, with ECOs to fix issues, ending in GDSII for tape-out.",
150: "Schoolbook multiplication of two n-digit numbers needs n^2 digit multiplications. Karatsuba splits each number into high and low halves, x = a B + b and y = c B + d, and computes ac, bd and (a + b)(c + d) with three multiplications instead of four, since ad + bc = (a + b)(c + d) - ac - bd. Applied recursively this gives about n^1.585 operations, which is much faster for large numbers, and is used in big-integer and cryptography hardware.",
151: "Exploit how predictable a trace is. Program counters mostly increase sequentially, so emit only branch outcomes (taken or not taken bits) and targets of indirect branches, as Arm CoreSight and Intel PT do; a decoder rebuilds the full flow from the program binary. Encode addresses as deltas from the previous address with variable-length encoding, and timestamps as deltas, then apply run-length or dictionary compression on repeated patterns. This compresses the stream by one to two orders of magnitude.",
152: "SPI is a synchronous, full-duplex serial bus with one master and one or more slaves. It uses SCLK (the clock from the master), MOSI (master out), MISO (master in) and an active-low chip select per slave. The master pulls a slave's chip select low and toggles SCLK; on each clock edge one bit shifts out on MOSI and one bit in on MISO, usually MSB first. The clock polarity and phase (CPOL and CPHA, modes 0 to 3) set which edge samples. There is no addressing or acknowledgement in the protocol.",
153: "The factory creates components and objects through type_id::create() instead of new, so a test can replace a type with a derived one without changing the environment: set_type_override_by_type() replaces it everywhere, set_inst_override_by_type() only at a given hierarchy path. config_db is a hierarchical database for passing configuration: set(context, path, name, value) stores a value (for example a virtual interface or a config object) and get() retrieves it in the build_phase of the component that matches the path.",
154: "Read the circuit and its intended function, then look for the usual bugs: wrong reset or reset polarity, incomplete sensitivity or latch inference, blocking assignments in sequential logic, width mismatches and truncation, missing default in case statements, multiple drivers, combinational loops, and clock-domain crossings without synchronisers. Rewrite it with always_ff for flops (non-blocking), always_comb for logic with defaults, typed logic signals and parameters, and check it with a testbench.",
155: "A typical test starts with class my_test extends uvm_test; followed by `uvm_component_utils(my_test) to register it with the factory (then the constructor function new(string name, uvm_component parent)). It ends with endclass. In run_phase, the test raises an objection, starts the sequence, and drops the objection; run_test() in the top module starts it.",
156: "UVM is a standard SystemVerilog class library and methodology for building reusable, constrained-random, coverage-driven testbenches, with components (agents, drivers, monitors, scoreboards), phasing, the factory, TLM communication and sequences. config_db is its hierarchical configuration database: higher levels set values such as virtual interfaces, configuration objects and knobs for a path, and lower components get them in their build_phase, so components stay reusable.",
157: "Abstraction simplifies the design so the formal tool can prove properties that would otherwise blow up in state space. Examples: cutting signals into free inputs (black-boxing a block), replacing a large counter or memory with a smaller abstract model, data-type reduction (prove for a few data bits instead of 64), and assume-guarantee decomposition. The abstraction must over-approximate behaviour, so a proof on it still holds for the real design; spurious counter-examples are refined away.",
158: "Formal verification proves properties mathematically for every possible input sequence from reset, using model checking or equivalence engines; it either gives a proof or a counter-example trace. Random (constrained-random simulation) verification samples the input space with stimulus and checks the responses, so it can only find bugs in the scenarios it happens to hit, measured by coverage. Formal is exhaustive but struggles with large designs and long sequences; simulation scales but is never complete.",
159: "The cone of influence of a property is the set of signals and state elements that can affect it, found by tracing backwards from the signals in the property through the logic to the inputs. Formal tools only analyse this cone and ignore everything else, which reduces the state space. Keeping properties local and cutting irrelevant logic keeps the cone small and the proof tractable.",
160: "Map each construct to hardware: continuous assigns and always @(*) blocks become combinational logic (operators become adders, comparators and gates; if and case become multiplexers or priority logic); always @(posedge clk) blocks become flip-flops with the logic feeding their D inputs; signals not assigned in every branch of combinational logic become latches; for loops unroll into repeated logic. Draw the resulting registers and logic between them.",
161: "Plot Vout against Vin. Region A (Vin < Vtn): NMOS off, PMOS linear, Vout = VDD. Region B: NMOS saturated, PMOS linear, output starts to fall. Region C (around the switching threshold VM): both saturated, very high gain, steep drop. Region D: NMOS linear, PMOS saturated. Region E (Vin > VDD - |Vtp|): PMOS off, NMOS linear, Vout = 0. VM is set by the PMOS-to-NMOS strength ratio; the slope of -1 points define VIL and VIH and the noise margins.",
162: "Connect an odd number of inverters in a loop: no stable state exists, so it oscillates with period T = 2 N td, where N is the number of stages and td the delay per inverter. To slow it down, add more stages (keeping the count odd), add load capacitance or use current-starved inverters (limit each stage's current with extra transistors controlled by a bias voltage, which makes it a VCO), or divide the output frequency with a counter.",
163: "Assuming both capacitors start uncharged, in series they get the same charge, so the voltages split in inverse proportion to capacitance: V across C2 = 5 V x C1 / (C1 + C2) = 5 x 10 / 11 = about 4.55 V, and about 0.45 V across C1. The smaller capacitor takes most of the voltage. If they were pre-charged, charge conservation on the node between them would decide the answer instead.",
164: "Write the function as a sum of the given minterms, then simplify. With 11 of 16 minterms for 4 variables, it is often easier to simplify the complement: group the 5 missing minterms (the maxterms) in a K-map to get F as a product of sums, or group the 1s with the largest possible blocks of 8, 4 and 2, using wrap-around adjacency. Check the result against a few minterms.",
165: "An FIR (finite impulse response) filter uses only current and past inputs: y[n] = sum of b_k x[n-k]. It is always stable, can have exactly linear phase, and is easy to pipeline, but needs many taps for sharp responses. An IIR (infinite impulse response) filter also feeds back past outputs, so it reaches a sharp response with far fewer coefficients and less hardware, but it can be unstable, has non-linear phase, and is sensitive to coefficient quantisation and overflow.",
166: "y = abc + a'bc' = b (ac + a'c') = b AND (a XNOR c). Draw an XNOR gate on a and c, then an AND gate with b. With basic gates: two AND3 gates (a, b, c) and (a', b, c') with inverters on a and c, feeding an OR2.",
167: "A real-time scope digitises the signal continuously at a sample rate several times its bandwidth, so it captures single-shot events. A sampling (equivalent-time) scope takes one sample per trigger at a slightly shifted delay each time and rebuilds a repetitive waveform, reaching very high bandwidths (tens of GHz) with slow ADCs, but it only works for repetitive signals such as eye diagrams.",
168: "Start by deciding whether the problem is in shift or capture. Run a chain integrity (flush) test first: if it fails in serial simulation, check scan connectivity, clocks and reset during shift, lockup latches and test-mode setup. Parallel simulation forces the chain values directly, so a pattern that passes in parallel but fails in serial points to shift problems; failures in both point to capture problems: timing (need SDF annotation), uninitialised X sources, clocking, constraints mismatch with ATPG, or bus contention. Compare the failing flop and cycle with the ATPG expected values.",
169: "Transmit a known PRBS pattern (PRBS7, PRBS31) through the link, and use a checker on the receive side that locks to the sequence and counts bit errors over a large number of bits. BER = errors / bits. To claim 1e-12 with 95% confidence and zero errors you need about 3e12 bits. Sweep jitter, attenuation and loss (stressed eye), and use the eye diagram, bathtub curves and on-die eye monitors to estimate margin; in simulation, check with statistical channel analysis.",
170: "A shared comparator is placed in the MBIST controller and compares data from several memories in turn: less area and fewer wires, but memories are tested sequentially (longer test time) and the routing of read data back to the controller can be long. Local comparators sit next to each memory: they allow parallel testing and faster diagnosis, at a higher area cost. The choice trades area against test time and power.",
171: "Typical scan DRC violations: clocks not controllable from top-level pins during test, asynchronous set or reset not controllable, uncontrolled latches or transparent latches in the scan path, combinational feedback loops, tri-state bus contention, flops not on a scan chain, X sources reaching scan flops (uninitialised memories or black boxes), clock-domain issues between chains, and shift-path problems. They are fixed with test points, test-mode muxes and constraints.",
172: "A static class member (a static property or method) belongs to the class rather than to each object: there is a single copy shared by all instances. A static property can count objects created or hold a shared configuration; static methods can only access static members and can be called with class_name::method() without creating an object. The UVM singleton patterns use this.",
173: "IR drop is the voltage lost along the power and ground network because of its resistance (V = I R), so cells see less than the nominal supply. Static IR drop uses average currents; dynamic IR drop uses simultaneous switching current peaks. Lower supply slows cells and can cause timing failures or functional errors. Fix it with a denser or wider power grid, more vias and pins, decap cells, and spreading high-activity logic.",
174: "A phase-locked loop generates a stable, high-frequency clock locked to a reference. A phase-frequency detector compares the reference with the divided output, a charge pump and loop filter turn the error into a control voltage, and a voltage-controlled oscillator produces the output; a divider by N in the feedback makes fout = N x fref. PLLs are used for clock multiplication, de-skewing, frequency synthesis and clock recovery.",
175: "A pull-up resistor ties a line weakly to VDD so it has a defined high level when nothing drives it, for example a floating input, a button, or an open-drain or open-collector bus such as I2C, where devices only pull low. Its value trades rise time (smaller is faster) against current and power when the line is pulled low.",
176: "Gate-level simulation runs the synthesized or post-layout netlist, often with SDF timing back-annotated. It catches synthesis-simulation mismatches, X-propagation and reset problems that RTL hides, scan and DFT behaviour, timing issues on paths that STA was told to ignore (false or multicycle paths, asynchronous logic), and power-up sequences, and it gives switching activity for power analysis.",
177: "Continuous assignments (assign y = a & b;) drive a net all the time: whenever a right-hand side changes, the net updates; they model combinational logic and wires. Procedural assignments happen inside always or initial blocks when that code executes, and assign to variables (reg or logic), which keep their value until assigned again. Procedural assignments can be blocking (=) or non-blocking (<=) and can model both combinational and sequential logic.",
178: "Write a class (or a module with tasks) that converts transactions to pin activity and back. Define a transaction class with the fields from the protocol; give the transactor a virtual interface to the block's signals; implement a drive task that waits for the bus to be ready, drives the fields with the right timing (valid, ready, data phases), and waits for completion, and a monitor task that samples the pins and builds transactions. Use a clocking block for clean sampling and a mailbox to exchange transactions with the testbench.",
179: "SystemVerilog extends Verilog for both design and verification. For design: logic, enum, struct, typedef, packages, interfaces, always_ff, always_comb and always_latch, and unique and priority case. For verification: classes and OOP, constrained random generation, functional coverage (covergroups), assertions (SVA), dynamic and associative arrays and queues, mailboxes and semaphores, fork-join variants, and clocking blocks. It is the base of UVM.",
180: "Cut-off (VGS < Vt): no channel, the transistor is off. Linear or triode (VGS > Vt and VDS < VGS - Vt): it acts like a voltage-controlled resistor. Saturation (VDS > VGS - Vt): the current depends mainly on VGS, so it acts as a current source. Digital circuits use the transistor as a switch, moving between cut-off and linear. Analog circuits bias it in saturation, where it gives gain (amplifiers, current mirrors, differential pairs).",
181: "Without the figure, the common case is a diode clipper or peak detector. For a series diode with R and C forming a peak detector: when Vi exceeds Vr + 0.7 V the diode conducts and Vr follows Vi - 0.7 V; when Vi falls, the diode turns off and the capacitor holds Vr, decaying with time constant RC = 1 us. For a shunt clipper, Vr follows Vi until it reaches 0.7 V (plus any reference) and then stays clamped. Sketch the transfer curve with the 0.7 V breakpoints.",
182: "As the sine rises from 0: near 0 V the NMOS is off and the PMOS is in its linear region (output high). As Vin passes Vtn the NMOS turns on in saturation while the PMOS is still linear. Around the switching threshold both are saturated. Then the NMOS moves into linear and the PMOS into saturation, and above VDD - |Vtp| the PMOS turns off with the NMOS linear (output low). On the falling half the sequence reverses.",
183: "Instruction pipelining divides instruction execution into stages (fetch, decode, execute, memory access, write-back) with registers between them, so while one instruction executes, the next is being decoded and the one after is being fetched. Throughput approaches one instruction per cycle and the clock can be faster, although each instruction's latency stays the same; hazards need forwarding, stalls and branch prediction.",
184: "Charge is conserved: the 1 C shares between two 1 F capacitors, so each ends at 0.5 V. The initial energy is 0.5 x 1 x 1^2 = 0.5 J; the final energy is 2 x 0.5 x 1 x 0.5^2 = 0.25 J. So 0.25 J, half of the initial energy, is dissipated in R, whatever the value of R (R only changes how fast it happens).",
185: "Both sequences repeat every 3 steps, and the second is the bitwise complement of the first. Use a mod-3 counter (2 flops counting 0, 1, 2) and decode it: count 0 gives 11, 1 gives 01, 2 gives 10. Then the outputs are y1 = (count != 1), y0 = (count != 2), and the second sequence is ~y1, ~y0. You can also build it directly as a 2-flop state machine whose state is the output pattern.",
186: "A false path is a timing path that can never be exercised functionally (for example between asynchronous clock domains with synchronisers, or test-only logic), so STA is told to ignore it with set_false_path. A half-cycle path launches on one edge of the clock and captures on the opposite edge (for example a negative-edge flop feeding a positive-edge flop), so it only has half a period for setup, but more room for hold.",
187: "If the parallel RC is driven directly by an ideal voltage source, the voltage across it is just Vi = Vp cos(t/RC). The likely intent is a source resistor R in series with the parallel RC: with omega = 1/RC the capacitor's impedance is -jR, the parallel combination is R/(1 + j), and the divider gives H = 1/(2 + j), so the output is Vp/sqrt(5) cos(t/RC - 26.6 degrees), about 0.45 Vp.",
188: "Draw the state diagram: one state per prefix of the pattern matched so far. On each input, go to the state for the longest suffix that is still a prefix (that handles overlaps). Choose Mealy (output on the transition, one state fewer, output in the same cycle) or Moore (output in a state, one cycle later, glitch-free). Code it with a state register in always_ff and next-state and output logic in always_comb with a default branch, then test with a stream containing overlapping matches.",
189: "They are the byte order of multi-byte words in memory. Big-endian stores the most significant byte at the lowest address (network order); little-endian stores the least significant byte first (x86, and most Arm systems). For 0x12345678 at address 0, big-endian memory holds 12 34 56 78 and little-endian holds 78 56 34 12. It matters when data crosses buses, files or networks.",
190: "Parasitics are the unwanted resistance, capacitance and inductance of wires and devices, extracted from the layout (SPEF) and added to timing and power analysis. EM (electromigration) is the gradual movement of metal atoms caused by high current density, which creates voids (opens) or hillocks (shorts) over time. IR drop is the voltage loss on the power grid due to its resistance. EM causes long-term wear-out failures; IR drop slows or breaks circuits; both are signed off with current-density and voltage-drop limits.",
191: "The gate-level netlist, the timing libraries (Liberty .lib files) for the cells at each corner, the constraints (SDC: clocks, input and output delays, exceptions), the parasitics (SPEF or SDF from extraction, or wire-load models before layout), and settings for operating conditions, derates or OCV and AOCV/POCV tables. For multi-mode multi-corner analysis, a set of each per scenario.",
192: "A timing arc is a delay path inside a cell or a net from one pin to another, described in the Liberty library. Combinational arcs go from an input to an output (positive unate, negative unate or non-unate). Sequential arcs go from the clock pin to the output (clock-to-Q), and timing-check arcs describe setup, hold, recovery and removal between data and clock. Net arcs connect a driver to its loads. STA traces paths by chaining arcs.",
193: "A function executes in zero simulation time, cannot contain delays or event controls, must return a value (or be void in SystemVerilog) and can only call other functions; it is used for combinational calculations. A task can consume time (#delay, @(posedge clk), wait), has no return value but can have output arguments, and can call functions and other tasks; it is used for stimulus sequences and bus protocols.",
194: "Top module (DUT, interface, clock and reset, run_test), then the test (configures and starts sequences), the environment (agents, scoreboard, coverage), the agents (sequencer, driver, monitor per interface), and the transactions (sequence items) and sequences. Without UVM: a top module, interface, program or testbench module, with a generator, driver, monitor, scoreboard and checker classes connected by mailboxes.",
195: "Inheritance: a class extends a base class and reuses its properties and methods (class burst_txn extends txn). Polymorphism: a base-class handle can point to a derived object, and virtual methods run the derived version at run time, so generic code works with any derived type. Abstraction: virtual classes and pure virtual methods define an interface that derived classes must implement, hiding implementation details. These are the basis of reusable testbenches and UVM.",
196: "Each time step is divided into regions. Preponed: values are sampled for assertions. Active: blocking assignments, continuous assignments and $display run. Inactive: #0 events. NBA: non-blocking assignments update. Observed: assertions are evaluated. Reactive: program blocks and assertion action blocks run. Postponed: $strobe and $monitor print final values. This ordering, with sampling before changes, is what lets clocking blocks and assertions avoid races with the design.",
197: "Build phases run first: build_phase (top-down: create components and get config), connect_phase (bottom-up: connect TLM ports), end_of_elaboration_phase (check topology), start_of_simulation_phase. Then run_phase, which is a task and consumes time (in parallel with the run-time sub-phases reset, configure, main and shutdown). Cleanup phases follow: extract_phase, check_phase, report_phase, final_phase. Phasing makes every component build, connect, run and report in a consistent order; objections control when run_phase ends.",
198: "Function phases take no simulation time: build (top-down), connect, end_of_elaboration, start_of_simulation, extract, check, report and final (bottom-up). Task phases consume time: run_phase, and the run-time sub-phases pre_reset, reset, configure, main and shutdown with pre and post variants, which run in parallel with run_phase. A phase ends when all raised objections are dropped. Build is top-down so parents create children; the others are bottom-up.",
199: "Type overrides replace every instance of a type: set_type_override_by_type(orig::get_type(), new::get_type()) or set_type_override_by_name(). Instance overrides replace only the objects created at a given hierarchy path: set_inst_override_by_type(path, orig::get_type(), new::get_type()) or by name. They only take effect for objects created with type_id::create() and must be set before the build_phase creates them. Instance overrides take priority over type overrides.",
}
